 17592186167575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166978 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "37fac92b-9502-52a2-8069-399fccbd8a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95078" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19a64e51-db17-58fb-852b-340494362842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167331 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56630566-56de-5b10-a1f2-1989c1203d78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167332 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167573 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167573 #edb/ref 1061 30861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167573 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6ab275a-61df-5648-811a-176fda344fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167574 #edb/ref 1061 30862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8627eaba-93e3-51d5-b350-72ee5af3e635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167575 #edb/ref 1061 30863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60b2ea1e-4b9a-5557-99ae-a83016e785a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167576 #edb/ref 1061 30864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26ddca41-ae16-5395-ae11-41c4d70303c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167577 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167577 #edb/ref 1061 30893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03fe551f-81c6-5c42-9baf-d6d12895ab47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167578 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167578 #edb/ref 1061 30894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ffafece-1c0b-50f7-a3b1-e1b2d1bada36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167579 #edb/ref 1061 30895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9731a04f-c2d1-5342-b940-0313b71ca1eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167697 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167697 #edb/ref 1061 32539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167697 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fdc61eff-9eab-552e-9092-f4040bdff21a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167697 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168061 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule for single variable functions $f=f(x)$ and $x=x(t)$ states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that $f = f(x,y),$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ are single-variable functions. How do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d t}$ in this case?\n\nThe functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are known as **intermediate variables.** They are independent variables for the function $f(x,y),$ but are themselves dependent on the variable $t.$\n\nOne way to consider how $f(x,y)$ depends on $t$ is to draw a tree diagram.\n\nStarting with $f(x,y),$ we draw a branch for each intermediate variable. Then, we draw a branch for both $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ which are both dependent on the variable $t.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc47e038ecaba62ae612a0ac0bc07067af232353fc4fc169bf4be57b3abbd1f.png)\n\nWe then label each branch with the derivative describing how the function changes due to a change in the variable. \n\n![](images/ec/ece6012e358b5b6920395df88acd8d7037a7b15bed1add1220365ba72bd24cbd.png)\n\nThen, traversing each branch, we compute the product of the derivatives along each branch and place the result at the end of the branch.\n\n![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nFinally, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we sum the results for each branch:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}\n = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThis result is called the **multivariable chain rule**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168061 #edb/ref 1065 4957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168061 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "80ad8a26-7d43-52d6-b0ba-e1d04283e5ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168061 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168062 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we apply the multivariable chain rule if the intermediate variables are also dependent on two variables? \n\nFor example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nf = f(x,y), \\qquad x=g(s,t), \\qquad y=h(s,t).\n$$\n\nHow do we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}?$ \n\nFirst, let's construct a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca524eda2e6be3354fcb72bed52fcaf22257073c3228d74850f8226f9ef0af6f.png)\n\nNow, since we're interested in $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s},$ we highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $s:$\n\n![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nIf we keep $t$ fixed and differentiate $f$ with respect to $s,$ then according to the chain rule for two intermediate variables and one independent variable, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we wanted to compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t},$ we would highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nKeeping $s$ fixed and differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168062 #edb/ref 1065 4958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168062 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58d189b1-31fb-5b1b-bc4c-cff6be5e2f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168062 #edb/ref 1067 "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Two Independent Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168063 #edb/ref 1064 "We can generalize the chain rule to a multivariable function containing an arbitrary number of intermediate and independent variables.\n\nConsider the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf = f(x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_m)\n$$\n\nwith $m$ independent variables and, for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ the variable \n\n$$\nx_i = g_i(t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_n)\n$$\n\nis a differentiable function of $n$ independent variables. Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t_j} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_1}{\\partial t_j} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_2}{\\partial t_j} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_m}{\\partial t_j}\n$$\n\nfor $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168063 #edb/ref 1065 4959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168063 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1ba7d2a4-05a6-5e62-8035-2610239633c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168063 #edb/ref 1067 "The Generalized Multivariable Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168088 #edb/ref 1064 "There is a special case of the multivariable chain rule when $f=f(x,y)$ and $y=g(x).$ \n\nNotice that $x$ is an independent variable here. Therefore, the chain rule becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}}_{⏟}}_{1}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168088 #edb/ref 1065 5184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168088 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "44effd03-02e7-5eb1-8732-ee1522afa147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168088 #edb/ref 1067 "A Special Case of the Multivariable Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281709 #edb/ref 1080 "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281709 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281709 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "021a846a-8825-55bf-a7b3-1dc5c5c5ff19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282314 #edb/ref 1080 "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282314 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282314 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283715 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283715 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283715 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8a1e1c7-9e8a-5f17-9500-4d7507d44b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283779 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283779 #edb/re